(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Accurate real-time glucose monitoring in critical illness enables better) Tj
T*
(insulin titration and avoids hypoglycaemia. We compared a wearable continuous monitoring) Tj
T*
(device against standard fingerstick and laboratory-based glucose measurement in septic) Tj
T*
(patients. Methods: Sixty ICU patients with confirmed sepsis were randomized to continuous) Tj
T*
(glucose monitoring \(CGM\) plus standard care or standard care alone. CGM readings were) Tj
T*
(compared against laboratory values using correlation analysis and Clarke error grid) Tj
T*
(analysis. Protocol adherence, measurement time, and hypoglycaemic episode detection were) Tj
T*
(monitored. Results: The CGM system demonstrated excellent agreement with reference methods) Tj
T*
(\(correlation coefficient r=0.986; p<0.001\) with mean absolute relative deviation of) Tj
T*
(11.44%. Clarke error grid analysis showed 91.9% of readings in clinically safe zone A and) Tj
T*
(8.1% in zone B. The CGM group achieved higher glycaemic protocol adherence \(73.91% versus) Tj
T*
(48.16%; p<0.001\) and required shorter measurement intervals \(41 seconds versus 64 seconds;) Tj
T*
(p<0.001\). Hypoglycaemic events were detected more frequently with CGM \(2.46% versus 0.28%;) Tj
T*
(p<0.001\). Time within target glucose range was 33.23% in CGM versus 26.26% in standard) Tj
T*
(care. Adverse events were not increased. Conclusion: CGM provides accurate real-time) Tj
T*
(glucose monitoring, improves protocol adherence, and enhances early hypoglycaemia) Tj
T*
(recognition in critically ill septic patients.) Tj
